  "strategy": "APPROACH AND RATIONALE\nThis approach leverages frequency-domain transforms coupled with adaptive sampling to iteratively refine a step function that minimizes the Erdos minimum overlap constant C5. The method is distinct from exhaustive grid searches or simple local optimization because it:\n\n1. Uses spherical transform to ensure full symmetry and remove degeneracy tendencies\n2. Bases initial guess on a Fourier-additive kernel with Gaussian decay\n3. Employs dynamic resolution expansion based on improvement rates\n4. Includes targeted local refinement using Chebyshev expansions\n5. Maintains validation across all scales\n\nThe key innovation is embedding the continuous optimization problem within a discrete search space while preserving continuity in the solution transfer between resolutions. This allows sequential improvements from coarse to fine scales without recomputing redundant information.\n\nMATHEMATICAL SPECIFICATION\nThe step function h(x) \u2208 [0,1] must satisfy \u222bh(x)dx=1 over [0,2]. The objective is:\n  C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\u2248 max\u2096 [max_lag(corr)] * (2/n)   (where corr = correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\"))\n\nThe discrete formulation:\n- n_is = {2^k for k=7 to 9} starting from parent's resolution (64 points base)\n- For each n_i, represent h with n_i samples on [0,2]\n- Constraints: h \u2208 [0,1]^n_i \u2227 \u2211h = n_i/2\n\nImplementation uses:\n- np.correlate for correlation-based evaluation\n- SLSQP with explicit gradient for local minimization\n- scipy.optimize.minimize for constrained optimization\n- Professor's kernel as base solution (if available)\n\nThe solution must be reproducible via:\n  h = np.zeros(n_i)\n  h = spherical_norm(h)\n  h = fourier_kernel(h)\n\nSEVERAL IMPLEMENTATION PROCEDURES\nINITIAL_CONSTRUCTION\n1. Input validation: Check n_is bounds and domain\n2. Validate initial_h_values if provided: ensure len==n_i_p (64)\n3. Build resolution ladder: n_list = [128, 256, 512] (if parent not available)\n\nTRANSFER_MATRICES\nTransfer between scales:\n  Low resolution h_lr \u2192 h_hr ->{Interpolate \u2192 Spherical projection \u2192 Fourier Filtering}\u2192 h_hr\n\nOPTIMIZATION PIPELINE\nFor each height size n_i:\n1. Stage 1: Initialize h with fourier_kernel\n2. Stage 2: Apply adaptive gradient descent if meeting improvement threshold (currently 0.001 per iteration)\n3. Stage 3: Primary optimization using lbfgs with bounds and explicit integral constraint\n4. Stage 4: Post-processing: Clip values, maintain integrals\n\nSEARCH CHOICES\n- Height resolution: expontial growth from 128 to 512\n- Optimization iterations: \n  Base (n=128): 100 iterations\n  n=256: 200 iterations\n  n=512: 300 iterations\n- Noise factor: bas_set at 0.05 during initialization\n- Stopping condition: 10 consecutive failures to improve OR time budget exhausted\n\nNUMERICAL ROBUSTNESS AND MEMORY USE\n- Memory: \n  h vectors: 32 bytes * 512 ~ 16 KB\n  Lower bounds: negligible\n  Residual storage: minimal during optimization\n- Convergence: Tracked via posterior improvement rate (<0.001 implies stall)\n- Solution repair: Uses normalized projection and gradient clipping\n\nRUN-TIME SAFEGUARDS\n1. Time tracking: \n   start_time = time.time()\n   elapsed = time.time() - start_time\n   if elapsed > budget_s: return best_solution\n\n2. Validation check: After optimization, perform two manual integrals of h to ensure validity\n\n3. Fallback procedure:\n   If no valid solution found during optimization:\n     h = np.ones(n_i) * 0.5\n     h = spherical_preprocessing(h, n_i)\n\n4. Objective evaluation:\n   Explicitly compute both maximum correlation and integral-normalized overlap\n\nRESCORER_FUNCTION\nCore evaluation code:\n   corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n   max_corr = np.max(corr)\n   c5 = max_corr * (2.0 / n_i)\n\nBEST_CANDIDATE_STORAGE\ntracks:\n   best_h - current best function\n   best_c5 - corresponding c5 value\n   best_n - resolution size\n\nRETURN_CONTRACT\nreturn (best_h, best_c5, best_n)\n\nGLOBAL CONSTRAINT_HANDLING\nIntegral constraint:\n  cons = {'type':'eq','fun':lambda h: np.sum(h) - n_i/2}\n\nLOCAL_NORMS\nh must be norm-consistent across scales:\n  ||h||_Erdos(n_i) = \u222bh dx = n_i/2\n  ||h||_bounded = max(h) - min(h) <= 1\n\nDERIVATIVE_HANDBLING\nGradient computation:\n   J = derivative(compute_c5, argnum=0)(h)\n   (computed numerically via central differences)\n\nDETAIL STEPS\n1. Initialize for each requested resolution n_i (default n_list=[128,256,512]):\n\n   Check parent availability:\n   if \"initial_h_values\" in globals():\n        base = initial_h_values\n        # First resolution must match parent's size (64)\n        if len(base) != 128: raise ValueError(\"Parent kernel length mismatch\")\n\n   Else: generate base with spherical_preprocessing\n\n   For each n_i in n_list:\n\n        # Set up memory\n        h = np.zeros(n_i)\n\n        # Base initialization (Fourier kernel)\n        h_kernel = fourier_kernel(h, n_i)\n\n        # Stage 1: Spherical normalization\n        h = spherical_projection(h_kernel)\n\n        # Apply primary optimization\n        try:\n            res = minimize_slsqp(compute_c5, h, n_i)\n        except OptimizationError:\n            # Fallback\n            h = gaussian_kernel(np.random.randn(n_i))\n\n        # Stage 2: Local refinement via adaptive gradient descent\n        if lower_improvement_rate(res.fun):\n            optimize_adaptive(h, n_i)\n\n        # Validate and repair\n        h = ensure_bounds(h, n_i)\n        h = enforce_integral(h, n_i)\n\n   Track best across resolutions.\n\n2. Utility functions:\n\n   def compute_c5(h, n):\n        \"Calculate the overlap bound given h and resolution n\"\n        corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n        max_val = np.max(corr)\n        return max_val * (2.0 / n)\n\n   def spherical_projection(h):\n        \"\"\"Space-normalize h by converting to spherical coordinates and adjusting symmetry\"\"\"\n        grad_mag = np.gradient(h)\n        return h / np.sqrt(np.sum(grad_mag**2))\n\n   def ensure_bounds(h, n):\n        \"\"\"Project to [0,1] maintaining integral\"\"\"\n        h_capped = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n        h_capped = project_to_feasible(h_capped, n/2.0)\n        return h_capped\n\n   def enforce_integral(h, n):\n        \"\"\"Adjust h using Gaussian smoothing to meet integral constraint\"\"\"\n        target = n/2.0\n        diff = np.sum(h) - target\n        if abs(diff) > 0.1:\n            h = h + (target - np.sum(h)) / n\n            h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n        return h\n\n3. Primary optimization function:\n\n   def minimize_slsqp(objective, x0, n):\n        \"\"\"Perform SLSQP optimization with explicit constraints\"\"\"\n        bounds = [(0,1)]*n\n        cons = {'type':'eq','fun': integral_constraint}\n        res = opt.minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=cons)\n\n        # Manual validity check\n        h_valid = ensure_bounds(res.x, n)\n        h_valid = enforce_integral(h_valid, n)\n        return h_valid\n\n4. Local refinement function:\n\n   def optimize_adaptive(h, n):\n        \"\"\"Refine solution with adaptive gradient descent\"\"\"\n        grad = central_gradient(compute_c5, h)\n        learning_rate = 0.05 / np.max(np.abs(grad))\n        for _ in range(30): # Limited iterations\n            h_new = h - learning_rate * grad\n            h_new = ensure_bounds(h_new, n)\n            h_new_val = compute_c5(h_new, n)\n            if current_c5 > h_new_val:\n                h = h_new\n                current_c5 = h_new_val\n            else:\n                break\n\n5. Fall-back solution generation:\n\n   def gaussian_kernel(h, n):\n        \"\"\"Generate initial guess using a reasonable base\"\"\"\n        x = np.linspace(0,2,n)\n        h_base = np.exp(-0.5 * np.power((x-1), 2))\n        h_base = normalize_integral(h_base, n/2.0)\n        h_base = project_to_feasible(h_base, n/2.0)\n        return h_base\n\n   def normalize_integral(h, target):\n        \"\"\"Scale h to specified integral\"\"\"\n        current_int = np.sum(h) * (2.0/n)\n        scale = target / current_int\n        return h * scale\n\nNUMERICAL INSTABILITY HANDLING\n- Minimal tolerance for integral constraint: 1e-6\n- Feature scaling factors: \n   learning_rate = 0.05 / max(|gradient|, 1e-3)\n- Check for NaN/inf in h values: converts to bounds\n\nTERMINATION AND RETURN\nIf timebudget exceeded: return current best solution\n\nSolution must satisfy:\n  0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n  \u222bh dx = 1\n\nVERIFY finally:\n   h_val = compute_c5(best_h, best_n)\n   assert(h_val < 0.3808)\n   assert(np.all((best_h>=0) & (best_h<=1)) and np.abs(1 - np.sum(best_h))<1e-4)\n   If local constraints not met: try fallback initial guess\n\nThe complete program implements these methods systematically, with explicit checks for resolution transfer and objective recalibration.",
